# The deployed directory is the project, but the hostname should only serve the page.
#
# This is the file that actually does it on a classic Pages project. A redirect rule is
# evaluated before the static asset is served, so these paths never reach their file --
# `.assetsignore` is never read by this uploader, and `_routes.json` only decides what
# reaches the Function, which falls back to the asset when no route of its own matches.
#
# 302, not 301: nothing here is secret, and a permanent redirect a browser caches forever
# is a worse thing to be wrong about than one it re-asks for.
#
# This file is never served itself -- the uploader withholds it, along with `_headers`,
# `_routes.json`, `_worker.js` and `functions/`.

/README.md      /  302
/CLAUDE.md      /  302
/schema.sql     /  302
/.assetsignore  /  302
/test/*         /  302

# Local wrangler dev state. Measured 2026-09-12: this WAS being served --
# /.wrangler/cache/wrangler-account.json returned 200 with the account name in it, and the
# directory also holds miniflare's D1/KV/cache SQLite files, which can carry whatever local
# dev last fetched. The uploader's fixed ignore list does NOT cover it, despite an earlier
# note in this repo claiming otherwise. The directory is gitignored, so nothing warns you.
# Deleting it before a deploy is the real fix; this rule is the backstop for the day someone
# runs `wrangler pages dev` and deploys without clearing it.
/.wrangler/*    /  302
